{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792609490,"nonce":"ZbDigVSh+FxwQrUo01BeJe8saZk=","message":"HoverDocumentation dependency investigation and progressive editor indexes\n\nFresh-server controlled probes in the sibling RayDE workspace, with the same discovered HXML and installed Haxe stdlib, isolate the import graph:\n\n- Empty root: 0.802s, 1 attempted file, 0 imports compiled.\n- Only rayde.lang.LspProtocol: 1.889s, 75 attempted files, 68 imports compiled, 766ms macro work.\n- Only ashui.layout.Element: 5.390s, 245 attempted files, 189 imports compiled, 3294ms macro work.\n- Only rayde.ext.MarkdownView: 11.608s, 477 attempted files, 279 imports compiled, 7061ms macro work.\n- Actual HoverDocumentation.hx: 11.583s, 485 attempted files, 286 imports compiled; 11.036s in import loading, 7.079s in macros, 22.5ms lowering the root itself.\n\nMarkdownView brings ashui.components.CodeView, which extends CodeEditor and imports the editor, CSS, input, reactive UI and SVG graph. Passing compact=true does not remove these dependencies from static analysis. Element itself has a broad layout/reactive dependency closure. These probes overlap and their costs are not additive. The project-wide ashui.ui.Markup.build hook also runs throughout the graph. Some build macros exhaust their budget; initialization still encounters unsupported Extensions and Xml hooks. These are separate macro-depth limitations.\n\nPortable BLADE editor indexes now retain named query responses independently of compiler graph lifetimes. Foreground requests can use them while the typing worker runs another document. Cold roots first publish source declarations and comments, then typed batches; only completed indexes persist. Symbol navigation/reference data is pooled once per symbol, while context-sensitive hovers remain per occurrence. Completed payloads and retained indexes have size/count bounds. Cache identity includes source inputs, unsaved overlays, configuration, stdlib and compiler/LSP build IDs. Input invalidation remains conservative across the project, not a fine-grained dependency engine.\n\nNative progressive probe during concurrent builds: declaration documentation available at 0.338s, responding in 0.93ms, while full analysis completed at 20.066s. A fresh server reused the persisted index at 0.125s and returned the hover in 5.14ms. Busy-machine observations, not isolated benchmarks. The different cold timing demonstrates that partial response latency is separate from total macro/dependency time.\n\nProtocol regression coverage verifies that a previously indexed page and the new root's source declaration can both answer hovers while a build macro waits on a release gate. Another test starts a second server, checks BLADE reuse without a checking cycle, then edits the buffer and verifies stale signatures are rejected. The verified protocol run passed 38 tests with 2 RayDE-specific tests ignored; 18 LSP unit tests also passed. Workspace/all-features/all-targets Clippy with -D warnings and fmt passed at this checkpoint.\n\nFurther verification is underway after restricting inferred placeholder field identities to concrete types: the full conformance comparison had identical case sets and only Issue7335 differed. Recording Dynamic on a placeholder field changed boxed increment behavior; dynamic fields must retain the runtime lookup representation.","files":null}]}